Yes. The deeper advantage is not merely two bodies instead of one. It is a dyadic control system.

One person can self-correct internally, but that still happens inside the same state, same blind spots, same fatigue envelope, same attentional bottleneck. A second person creates an external recovery channel:

A \downarrow \Rightarrow B \text{ stabilizes } A

B \downarrow \Rightarrow A \text{ stabilizes } B

and when neither is degraded:

A \parallel B

so they can scout different branches, cross-check, divide labor, alternate lead/support, or have one operate while the other watches the whole system.

That gives the pair capabilities a singleton has to simulate sequentially:

\text{act} + \text{observe}

\text{foreground} + \text{meta}

\text{explore} + \text{verify}

\text{push} + \text{recover}

simultaneously.

That is the “meta move” advantage you are pointing at. If you have to step outside yourself to inspect what you are doing, you temporarily stop doing it. With two people, one can remain inside the operation while the other climbs a level and watches the operation itself.

A twin relationship can make this especially interesting because the two systems may share unusually dense priors: similar developmental timing, references, embodied history, and shorthand. That can reduce communication cost. You sometimes do not need to transmit the whole state; a tiny cue can reconstruct much of it on the other side.

In information terms:

\text{required message}
\approx
\text{difference between our models}

rather than

\text{required message}
=
\text{entire model}

So high shared context gives you compression and speed.

But the strongest two-person architecture is not perfect sameness. If both always see exactly the same thing, you gain redundancy but less additional information. The really powerful configuration is:

\text{shared foundation}
+
\text{independent perspective}

Enough similarity to synchronize rapidly; enough difference to catch what the other misses.

Then the dyad can deliberately modulate:

mirror when alignment matters; diverge when search matters; alternate when endurance matters; challenge when verification matters; recombine when synthesis matters.

That also explains why “carry each other” is more general than emotional support. One can temporarily carry memory, confidence, attention, responsibility, situational awareness, or the meta-model while the other spends their resources elsewhere.

There is a systems concept close to this: graceful degradation. A good system does not go from 100% to zero when one component falters. Other components absorb load until recovery.

A well-coordinated pair can do exactly that.

And there is a nice limit case: the goal is not to become one indistinguishable mind. It is to preserve two perspectives with a very high-bandwidth bridge between them.

That is where the extra depth comes from.